"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][10]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M10-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M10-L1 – Supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement learning<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Foundations of machine learning (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L1<\/a>,\nM4, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish learning tasks by\ntheir feedback and objectives and formulate suitable applications.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a rule to a learnable task<\/h2>\n<p>A company wants to detect the failure of a cooling unit early. A\nhard-coded rule triggers an alarm at temperatures above 80 °C. A\nlearning model, by contrast, can infer relationships between load,\ntemperature patterns and subsequent faults from historical measurements.\nThe choice of model family, data and learning objective shapes the\nmodel. It is neither an independent authority on knowledge nor\nnecessarily a neural network.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three forms of feedback<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001622_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Supervised learning uses measurements and failure labels, unsupervised learning only measurements, and reinforcement learning state and reward with feedback through the environment.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Supervised learning uses measurements and\nfailure labels, unsupervised learning only measurements, and\nreinforcement learning state and reward with feedback through the\nenvironment.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Three types of learning in operation.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In supervised learning, examples\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x_i,y_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconsist of features and target values. Classification predicts a\ncategory, such as failure within 24 hours (yes/no). Regression estimates\na numerical value, such as remaining operating hours. In classification,\nthe model may first output a probability before a decision is made.<\/p>\n<p>Unsupervised learning operates without the prescribed target\nvariable. It searches for structures in the measurement vectors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsuch as groups of similar operating states or a small number of\ninformative coordinates. A cluster does not automatically correspond to\na real fault type. The assignment requires specialist assessment.<\/p>\n<p>In reinforcement learning, an agent interacts with an environment. It\nreceives a state (or an observation), chooses an action and receives\nfeedback in the form of a reward. The strategy, also called a policy,\nspecifies how actions are selected. The objective may be the expected\ndiscounted return:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_t=\\sum_{k=0}^{T-t-1}\\gamma^k r_{t+k+1},\\qquad 0\\le\\gamma\\le1.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For a finite episode with end time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand rewards\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nweights later rewards. Unlike one-off classification, actions often\nchange future observations. The diagram compares the different\ninformation flows using the cooling unit as an example.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hybrid forms and choice of learning task<\/h2>\n<p>Self-supervised learning constructs targets from raw data, for\nexample by predicting a masked sensor measurement from its surroundings.\nSemi-supervised learning combines a small number of labelled examples\nwith many unlabelled ones. An unsupervised method can generate features\nfor a subsequent supervised model. What matters, therefore, is the\nfunction of a learning step, not merely the label of the entire\nsystem.<\/p>\n<p>Predicting a cooling failure is different from controlling the\ncooling system. Control also involves energy consumption, safety limits\nand delayed effects. A deployed policy must comply with permissible\nactions; merely maximising the reward does not guarantee a safe\ninstallation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>An agent receives 2, 0 and 4 reward points in sequence with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate the return at the start. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>0.5<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+0.5\\cdot0+0.5^2\\cdot4=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit is 6. A dataset of previously measured temperatures and fault\nclasses, by contrast, would form the basis for supervised learning; a\ndataset containing only temperature patterns could be used for cluster\nanalysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/intro-to-ml/what-is-ml\">Google:\nWhat is Machine Learning?<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262039246/reinforcement-learning/\">Sutton/Barto:\nReinforcement Learning, 2nd edition (2018)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/\">James et al.: An\nIntroduction to Statistical Learning with Applications in Python\n(2023)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M10-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M10-L2 – Training, validation and test data<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Foundations of machine learning (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M3, M5, M6, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Split datasets appropriately for\nthe deployment scenario and distinguish the three subsets by their\nfunctions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why a good training score is insufficient<\/h2>\n<p>A model is intended to predict the remaining operating time of\ndevices from measurements. The observed examples are used to determine\nits parameters. How well this prediction rule works on other devices\ncannot be reliably assessed using the same observations. Even a method\nwithout a meaningful rule could memorise the known targets and achieve a\ntraining error of zero.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three distinct tasks<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001723_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Seventy per cent training, fifteen per cent each for validation and test; selection iterates only with development data, followed by final testing.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Seventy per cent training, fifteen per\ncent each for validation and test; selection iterates only with\ndevelopment data, followed by final testing.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Training, validation and test.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>One possible split of 1,000 independent observations is 700 for\ntraining, 150 for validation and 150 for final testing. These sizes are\na teaching example, not a general prescription. Training estimates model\nparameters. Validation supports decisions about the model family,\nfeatures, regularisation and other settings. The test is used only after\nthe selection has been finalised. If optimisation resumes after a poor\ntest score, that test has effectively become a validation set.<\/p>\n<p>The diagram shows the 70/15/15 per cent split and the permitted\ninformation flows. After selection, a model can be refitted on the\ntraining and validation data; the test remains unchanged. Before\ndeployment, it is also necessary to check whether the sample adequately\nrepresents the intended application.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The unit of splitting<\/h2>\n<p>When there are several measurements per device, rows from the same\ndevice are often highly similar. If the aim is to assess a new device,\nits rows must be assigned together to exactly one subset. For prediction\ninto the future, temporal boundaries are more suitable; all features\nmust be available at prediction time. Overlapping time windows or\ntargets collected later may require an additional gap around the split\nboundaries. For independent classification examples, stratified sampling\nhelps keep class proportions approximately stable. It does not replace\ngroup-based or temporal splitting.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.model_selection <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> GroupShuffleSplit<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ids <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.repeat(np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>), <span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>).reshape(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>tr, rest <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">next<\/span>(GroupShuffleSplit(n_splits<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, test_size<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.3<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                                 random_state<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>).split(X, groups<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>ids))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>v, t <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">next<\/span>(GroupShuffleSplit(n_splits<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, test_size<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                              random_state<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">13<\/span>).split(X[rest], groups<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>ids[rest]))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>val, test <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> rest[v], rest[t]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>sets <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"bu\">set<\/span>(ids[i]) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> i <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (tr, val, test)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> [<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(i) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> i <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> sets] <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">14<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(<span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> a <span class=\"op\">&amp;<\/span> b <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> j, a <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">enumerate<\/span>(sets) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> b <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> sets[j<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The random seeds make the particular split reproducible. With few\ngroups, however, the performance estimate remains uncertain; further\ngroup-based assessments may be useful.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A camera produces ten almost identical images per product. The aim is\nto recognise previously unseen products. Why is a random image-level\nsplit risky? Solution: images of the same product can end up on both\nsides; generalisation to new products is consequently overestimated.\nGroup by product ID, not image number.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/dividing-datasets\">Google:\nDatasets – Dividing the original dataset<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html\">scikit-learn:\nCommon pitfalls and recommended practices<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation.html\">scikit-learn:\nCross-validation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M10-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M10-L3 – Loss functions and empirical risk<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Foundations of machine learning (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate losses, distinguish\nempirical from expected risk and explain the role of regularisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Loss as a mathematical learning objective<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001529_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two log-loss curves for y equal to one and zero; highlighted losses at p equal to 0.2 and 0.8 for y equal to one.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two log-loss curves for y equal to one\nand zero; highlighted losses at p equal to 0.2 and 0.8 for y equal to\none.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Log loss for binary targets.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A loss function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell(y,f_\\theta(x))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nassigns a numerical assessment to a prediction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\theta(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor remaining operating times in hours, absolute deviations are easy to\ninterpret; squared deviations penalise larger errors more strongly. For\na binary target variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\in\\{0,1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an estimated probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\in(0,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe logarithmic loss is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell(y,p)=-y\\ln p-(1-y)\\ln(1-p).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A wrong, highly confident prediction therefore costs considerably\nmore than an uncertain one. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe loss is about 0.223; for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nabout 1.609. The diagram shows the two loss curves. In numerical code,\nstable calculations from logits are preferable; otherwise, the boundary\ncases zero and one can lead to undefined logarithms.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sample and target population<\/h2>\n<p>The expected risk of a specified prediction rule is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(\\theta)=\\mathbb E[\\ell(Y,f_\\theta(X))]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince the joint distribution of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis usually unknown, the available sample is used:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>R<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/munderover><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>∈<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msub><mover><mi>R<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat R_n(\\theta)=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\ell(y_i,f_\\theta(x_i)),\\qquad \\hat\\theta\\in\\arg\\min_{\\theta\\in\\Theta}\\widehat R_n(\\theta).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The minimum depends on the model class\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the sample. For a rule specified in advance, the sample mean is an\nunbiased estimate of the risk for identically distributed observations\nwith finite expectation. After selecting a minimiser on the same\ndataset, the training score is generally too optimistic. This is why the\nevaluation data from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L2<\/a>\nare necessary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Choosing a practical objective<\/h2>\n<p>Classification error with 0/1 loss is piecewise constant and poorly\nsuited to many optimisation methods. A differentiable surrogate loss\nsuch as log loss, by contrast, provides continuous feedback.\nRegularisation supplements the objective function, for example with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\|\\theta\\|_2^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(see <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L7<\/a>).\nThe value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis selected using validation. The training objective and operational\nevaluation metric may differ, but must be appropriate for the same\ntask.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> p <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.3<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>loss <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span>(y<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.log(p) <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.log1p(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(loss.mean(), <span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.log(<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.7<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Two models have errors in hours of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0,6)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,2,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCompare mean absolute error and mean squared error. Solution: the mean\nabsolute error is 2 h for both, while the mean squared errors are 12 h²\nand 4 h². If extreme deviations are to be weighted more heavily, the\nsecond model is preferable here.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://stat.cmu.edu/~cshalizi/sml/21/lectures/03/lecture-03.html\">CMU:\nEmpirical Risk Minimization (2021)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html\">scikit-learn:\nMetrics and scoring<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/\">James et al.: An\nIntroduction to Statistical Learning with Applications in Python\n(2023)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M10-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M10-L4 – Feature engineering, scaling and data leakage<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Foundations of machine learning (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M3, M5, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Construct features from\navailable raw data and create a leakage-free processing pipeline.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Representing a situation<\/h2>\n<p>A cooling unit reports temperature and power consumption every\nminute. Instead of a long sequence of individual values, the mean,\nmaximum and slope over the last hour can serve as features. This choice\nchanges what a model can detect. If future measurements are used in a\nsymmetric time window, the feature is inadmissible for real-time\nprediction. The feature definition must therefore specify its\nrelationship to the query time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scaling and learned transformations<\/h2>\n<p>For a numerical feature, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_{\\mathrm{train}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the mean and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{\\mathrm{train}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe standard deviation of the training values. Standardisation is given\nby<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=\\frac{x-\\mu_{\\mathrm{train}}}{s_{\\mathrm{train}}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here we use the empirical variance with divisor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the training values 10, 12 and 14,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.633<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=\\sqrt{8/3}\\approx1.633<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA later value of 18 becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.674<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z\\approx3.674<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt need not lie within the earlier range. For a constant column,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\na defined special treatment is necessary. Models based on distances or\nregularisation are often sensitive to scaling; tree-based methods\nusually do not require it. The units and interpretability of the raw\nfeatures are retained in the data contract.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data leakage and pipelines<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001725_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Training values determine mean 12 and standard deviation approximately 1.633. The new value 18 uses the same parameters without refitting the transformation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Training values determine mean 12 and\nstandard deviation approximately 1.633. The new value 18 uses the same\nparameters without refitting the transformation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Scaling without data leakage.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Leakage occurs, for example, when a workshop diagnosis made after\nfailure is used as an input to predict that failure. Imputation, feature\nselection based on targets, dimensionality reduction and scaling must\nlikewise not be fitted using validation or test data. The diagram shows\nthe split taking place before the transformation is fitted. In\ncross-validation (<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L6<\/a>),\nthe entire pipeline is refitted in each round.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.preprocessing <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> StandardScaler<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.pipeline <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> make_pipeline<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.linear_model <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Ridge<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">10.<\/span>], [<span class=\"fl\">12.<\/span>], [<span class=\"fl\">14.<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">5.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>pipe <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), Ridge(alpha<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>pipe.fit(X, y)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>z <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> pipe[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>].transform([[<span class=\"fl\">18.<\/span>]])[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(z, <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(pipe[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>].mean_[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>], <span class=\"fl\">12.<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Categorical data can be encoded as indicator columns; previously\nunseen categories require a defined treatment. Missing values can\nthemselves carry information about measurement failures. The chosen rule\nmust be consistent between training and deployment. A pipeline does not\nautomatically detect a feature that is inadmissible for domain-specific\nreasons.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A table contains service age, temperature and repair costs recorded\nonly after a failure. Which values are suitable for prediction before\nfailure? Solution: age and temperature measured up to that point are in\nprinciple suitable; later repair costs are not. Highly correlated\nfeatures can be suspicious for precisely this reason.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html\">scikit-learn:\nCommon pitfalls and recommended practices<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation.html\">scikit-learn:\nCross-validation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M10-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M10-L5 – The bias–variance trade-off and generalisation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Foundations of machine learning (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>,\nM6, M8, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the squared-error\ndecomposition and assess the influence of bias and variance using an\nexample.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two distinct causes of poor predictions<\/h2>\n<p>An overly simple model of the relationship between outside\ntemperature and cooling performance may consistently produce similar but\nsystematically incorrect values. A highly flexible model, by contrast,\nmay change considerably when data collection is repeated. Systematic\ndeviation and sensitivity to the choice of sample are distinct\nproperties of a learning method.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decomposing expected squared error<\/h2>\n<p>For a fixed input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m(x)=\\mathbb E[Y\\mid X=x]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2(x)=\\operatorname{Var}(Y\\mid X=x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDenote the rule learned from random training data\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLet a new target value be independent of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>f<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar f(x)=\\mathbb E_D[f_D(x)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe following holds for finite second moments:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>f<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb E_{D,Y\\mid x}[(Y-f_D(x))^2]=\\sigma^2(x)+(m(x)-\\bar f(x))^2+\\operatorname{Var}_D(f_D(x)).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>To derive this, decompose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>f<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>f<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y-f_D=(Y-m)+(m-\\bar f)+(\\bar f-f_D)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen squaring, the mixed expectations vanish: the first and third terms\nin brackets have expectation zero and are independent given\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe three remaining terms are the conditional residual variance, squared\nsystematic deviation (bias²) and model variance. Further averaging over\nthe inputs yields an overall statement. This particular identity does\nnot apply unchanged to other losses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparing two learning rules<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001527_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Stacked error components: rule A has residual variance one, squared bias four and variance two thirds; rule B has residual variance one, squared bias zero and variance six.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Stacked error components: rule A has\nresidual variance one, squared bias four and variance two thirds; rule B\nhas residual variance one, squared bias zero and variance\nsix.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Bias, variance and residual error.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m(x)=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2(x)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrule A produces 7, 8 and 9 in three equally likely training situations.\nIts mean is 8, its bias² is 4 and its variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRule B produces 7, 10 and 13. Its mean is 10, bias² is zero and variance\nis 6. The expected squared errors are therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>17<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5.67<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">17/3\\approx5.67<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand 7. The diagram compares the same synthetic situations.<\/p>\n<p>Flexibility can decrease bias and increase variance, but a U-shaped\nerror curve is not a universal law. Data volume, regularisation and the\nlearning algorithm affect both components. Empirically, large\ndifferences between training and validation errors often indicate\ngeneralisation problems; however, data shift or poor data quality can\nproduce similar patterns. Without knowledge of the true data\ndistribution, the three terms cannot be measured directly and\nexactly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A third rule always produces 11 regardless of the sample. Calculate\nthe expected error at the same input. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+(10-11)^2+0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt this single point it is better than the other rules. Whether this\nholds over the entire input space remains open.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.teach.cs.toronto.edu/~csc311notes/bias_variance_decomposition/bv.html\">University\nof Toronto CSC311: Bias-Variance Decomposition<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/\">James et al.: An\nIntroduction to Statistical Learning with Applications in Python\n(2023)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M10-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M10-L6 – Cross-validation and hyperparameter search<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Foundations of machine learning (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L2<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Perform k-fold cross-validation\nand select hyperparameters without using the final test.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Repeated evaluation with limited data<\/h2>\n<p>A single validation sample may happen to contain particularly easy\ncases. In k-fold cross-validation, the development data are divided into\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndisjoint blocks. On each run, one block is held out for validation and\nthe model is trained anew on the remaining blocks. Each block is used\nfor validation exactly once. A separate test is used only at the\nend.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Folds and comparison measures<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001616_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Five equally sized blocks in five rounds; a different block is used for validation and the other four for fitting.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Five equally sized blocks in five rounds;\na different block is used for validation and the other four for\nfitting.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Five-fold cross-validation.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>With five equally sized folds, each model yields five validation\nscores\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_1,\\ldots,v_5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir arithmetic mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar v=k^{-1}\\sum_j v_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor unequal block sizes, aggregation must suit the objective: for an\naverage loss per observation, weights can reflect the number of cases;\nfor equal weighting of sites, a group mean is conceivable instead.\nNon-linear measures such as F1 calculated on pooled predictions can\ndiffer from the mean of individual F1 scores. The diagram shows five\nequally sized folds with their changing roles.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hyperparameters and selection bias<\/h2>\n<p>Parameters arise during fitting; hyperparameters specify how fitting\ntakes place, such as the strength of ridge regularisation or tree depth.\nA grid search checks all combinations specified in advance. A random\nsearch draws combinations from a search space and may be more economical\nin many dimensions. Each variant must be evaluated on the same splits.\nAll learned preprocessing steps belong in the pipeline.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.model_selection <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> KFold, GridSearchCV<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.pipeline <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> make_pipeline<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.preprocessing <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> StandardScaler<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.linear_model <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Ridge<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.linspace(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">40<\/span>).reshape(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x[:, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.sin(<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x[:, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>pipe <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), Ridge())<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>cv <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> KFold(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>, shuffle<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>, random_state<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>search <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> GridSearchCV(pipe, {<span class=\"st\">&#39;ridge__alpha&#39;<\/span>: [<span class=\"fl\">.01<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">10.<\/span>]},<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                      cv<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>cv, scoring<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;neg_mean_squared_error&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>search.fit(x, y)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(search.cv_results_[<span class=\"st\">&#39;mean_test_score&#39;<\/span>]) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> search.best_params_[<span class=\"st\">&#39;ridge__alpha&#39;<\/span>] <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [<span class=\"fl\">.01<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">10.<\/span>]<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The negative MSE follows the API convention that higher scores are\nbetter. The four candidates require 20 fold fits and, with the default\nrefit, one additional fit. The best internal score is not an independent\nfinal assessment. Without a separate test, nested cross-validation can\nevaluate the entire selection procedure in outer folds. For temporal and\ngrouped data, the inner split must also account for these dependencies.\nThe fold scores are dependent because of overlapping training sets;\ntheir standard deviation is not a directly valid estimate of the\nstandard error.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Three candidates each have four validation errors: A=(3,4,2,3),\nB=(2,3,3,2), C=(1,6,1,6). Which has the smallest mean? Solution: A=3,\nB=2.5, C=3.5. B wins according to the specified criterion; independent\nevaluation is still missing.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation.html\">scikit-learn:\nCross-validation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/grid_search.html\">scikit-learn:\nTuning the hyper-parameters<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html\">scikit-learn:\nCommon pitfalls and recommended practices<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M10-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M10-L7 – Classification metrics and decision thresholds<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Foundations of machine learning (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4, M6, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate confusion matrices and\nclassification metrics and relate decision thresholds to error\ncosts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Four possible outcomes<\/h2>\n<p>A model flags potentially defective devices before maintenance.\nPositive here means actually defective. A correct warning is TP, a false\nwarning FP, a missed defect FN and a device correctly not flagged TN.\nThe confusion matrix arranges these categories by actual and predicted\nstate. Changing the positive class changes several metrics; the class\nmust therefore always be specified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparing metrics<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001610_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Matrix with actual state in rows and prediction in columns: 24 TP, 6 FN, 16 FP, 154 TN; metrics alongside.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Matrix with actual state in rows and\nprediction in columns: 24 TP, 6 FN, 16 FP, 154 TN; metrics\nalongside.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Confusion matrix for defect alerts.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For 200 devices, suppose there are 24 TP, 16 FP, 6 FN and 154 TN (see\ndiagram). Overall accuracy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>154<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>200<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.89<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(24+154)/200=0.89<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOther metrics are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Precision<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Recall<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{Precision}=\\frac{TP}{TP+FP}=0.60,\\qquad \\text{Recall}=\\frac{TP}{TP+FN}=0.80,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Specificity<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>154<\/mn><mn>170<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.906<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>48<\/mn><mn>70<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.686<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{Specificity}=\\frac{TN}{TN+FP}=\\frac{154}{170}\\approx0.906,\\qquad F_1=\\frac{2TP}{2TP+FP+FN}=\\frac{48}{70}\\approx0.686.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Balanced accuracy is the mean of recall and specificity,\napproximately 0.853 here. A model making exclusively negative\npredictions already achieves 85% ordinary accuracy, but no positive\nrecall. If the denominator is zero, the corresponding rate is undefined;\nsoftware output conventions must be documented explicitly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Thresholds and decisions<\/h2>\n<p>For a predicted defect probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na threshold\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermines when a warning is issued. With correctly calibrated\nprobabilities, constant costs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{FP}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{FN}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand zero cost for correct decisions, the warning is cheaper if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-p)C_{FP}\\le pC_{FN}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=C_{FP}/(C_{FP}+C_{FN})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith a cost of 20 euros for a false alarm and 180 euros for a missed\ndefect, the threshold is 0.1. Capacity constraints and additional costs\nmay require a different decision rule.<\/p>\n<p>An ROC curve plots recall against the false positive rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">FP/(FP+TN)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor different thresholds. A precision–recall curve shows the quality of\npositive assignments; its baseline depends strongly on class prevalence.\nROC AUC measures ranking, not probability calibration. The threshold is\nset using development data, not on the basis of subsequent test\nresults.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.metrics <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> confusion_matrix, f1_score, balanced_accuracy_score<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">30<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">170<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>pred <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">24<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">16<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">154<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> confusion_matrix(y, pred, labels<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]).tolist() <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [[<span class=\"dv\">154<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">16<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">24<\/span>]]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(f1_score(y,pred),<span class=\"dv\">48<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">70<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(balanced_accuracy_score(y,pred),(<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">154<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">170<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>With a lower threshold, warnings are additionally issued for four\npreviously missed defects and twelve previously correctly unflagged\ndevices. Solution: TP=28, FN=2, FP=28, TN=142; recall=28/30,\nprecision=28/56=0.5. At the costs above, total error costs decrease from\n1,400 to 920 euros.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html\">scikit-learn:\nMetrics and scoring<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://stat.cmu.edu/~cshalizi/sml/21/lectures/03/lecture-03.html\">CMU:\nEmpirical Risk Minimization (2021)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M10-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M10-L8 – Regression metrics and baselines<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Foundations of machine learning (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M5, M8, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret regression metrics\nwith their units and assess a model against an admissible baseline.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which errors matter in practice?<\/h2>\n<p>A service predicts repair duration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin hours. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobserved values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand residuals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_i=y_i-\\hat y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncommonly used metrics are:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msubsup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{MAE}=\\frac1n\\sum_i|e_i|,\\quad \\mathrm{MSE}=\\frac1n\\sum_i e_i^2,\\quad \\mathrm{RMSE}=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{MSE}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>MAE and RMSE have the unit of the target value, whereas MSE has the\nsquared unit. RMSE is more sensitive to individual large errors. The\nmean of signed residuals indicates possible systematic underestimation\nor overestimation; positive and negative errors can cancel each other\nout. Analysis by device group and load range supplements the overall\nscore.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparing the same cases<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001681_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Three evaluation values of 2, 4, 6 hours, model values of 3, 3, 5 and baseline 4, 4, 4; lines connect observations with predictions.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three evaluation values of 2, 4, 6 hours,\nmodel values of 3, 3, 5 and baseline 4, 4, 4; lines connect observations\nwith predictions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Regression errors against a baseline.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Three repairs actually take 2, 4 and 6 hours. A model predicts 3, 3\nand 5 hours. A comparison model always predicts 4 hours; assume that\nthis value is already known from the training data. The diagram shows\nall deviations. For the model, MAE=1 h and RMSE=1 h. For the constant\nbaseline, MAE=4/3 h and\nRMSE=<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.633<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{8/3}\\approx1.633<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nh.<\/p>\n<p>A dimensionless measure is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^2=1-\\frac{\\sum_i(y_i-\\hat y_i)^2}{\\sum_i(y_i-\\bar y)^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the mean of the actual evaluation values. In the example, numerator\nand denominator are 3 and 8, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.625<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^2=0.625<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe measure can become negative on new data if the sum of squared errors\nexceeds that of the constant evaluation-set mean. For constant targets,\nthe denominator is zero. The evaluation-set mean in the mathematical\nreference measure must not be confused with a prediction admissible in\noperation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Baselines before complex models<\/h2>\n<p>For squared loss, the training mean is the optimal constant estimate\non the training data; for absolute loss, it is the median. For\ntime-ordered data, the last available observation or the value from the\nprevious season may be more appropriate. Every baseline must use the\nsame information cutoff and evaluation cases. A percentage error such as\nMAPE is undefined when the target value is zero and often of little\nsignificance when magnitudes are very small. MAE is frequently easier to\ninterpret in these cases.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.metrics <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error, r2_score<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">6.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> pred <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">5.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>base <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.full(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>)  <span class=\"co\"># aus Entwicklungsdaten festgelegt<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(mean_absolute_error(y,pred),<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(mean_squared_error(y,base),<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(r2_score(y,pred),<span class=\"fl\">.625<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>An improvement in a small sample may be due to chance. A comparison\nshould consider errors on identical cases and uncertainty in the\ndifferences, for example by paired bootstrap for independent cases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Another model predicts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,4,8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours. Solution: the residuals are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,0,-2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nMAE=4/3 h, MSE=8/3 h² and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mean signed error is zero, although two predictions are wrong by two\nhours. The first model variant is better here according to both error\nmeasures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html\">scikit-learn:\nMetrics and scoring<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/\">James et al.: An\nIntroduction to Statistical Learning with Applications in Python\n(2023)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
